{"step_index":166,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T02:05:08Z","input_tokens":2283,"cache_read_tokens":267023,"output_tokens":15424,"thinking":"The goal is to establish a comprehensive taxonomy for app performance and stability mechanisms within the codebase. The approach will involve a strictly MECE (Mutually Exclusive, Collectively Exhaustive) and architecturally coherent categorization of the mechanisms. This taxonomy should be derived directly from the available code.\n\n\nInitial assessment suggests categorizing mechanisms by the four domains: Execution/Compilation/Startup Performance, GC/Memory Management, Concurrency/Low Latency, and Stability/Observability/Diagnostics. Each domain likely contains multiple distinct mechanisms warranting individual tasks and wikis. This decomposition aims for a balance between granularity and architectural coherence within the code. Considering a preliminary division into 16 core mechanisms appears reasonable; however, this is a starting point, and further code analysis might necessitate refinements to this structure.\n\n\nFocus shifts to validating the proposed 16 mechanisms against the codebase, beginning with the first domain: Execution, Compilation, and Startup Performance. The goal is to ensure each identified mechanism has corresponding code modules, preventing any unsupported assumptions. The first task examines the tiered execution model, requiring verification across the interpreter and JIT compiler modules. Specific files within the provided paths will be cross-referenced.\n\n\nThe tiered execution model encompasses an interpreter, a baseline JIT, and an optimizing JIT with OSR. The interpreter offers zero-overhead ABI transitions. Baseline JIT uses hotness counters. Optimizing JIT employs inline caches and class hierarchy analysis.\n\nProfile-Guided Optimization (PGO) is the second mechanism. It utilizes runtime hotness profiling and AOT compilation, with cloud-based